#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134105 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133802 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bc1e31e-5794-5ae3-8620-6bcaadde6a15" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System With Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ae9b053-73a2-5eb3-b597-87d039af513d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266808} {:answer/id #uuid "72fb8006-32f8-5a4b-b420-08bb7d248ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267152} {:answer/id #uuid "85f588e5-6773-5642-8c30-504849a49eca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267324} {:answer/id #uuid "8733d84d-cb14-50b6-a119-5848b9c83b85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267339} {:answer/id #uuid "de87d864-0f52-58a0-9af7-835670ea9d69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72fb8006-32f8-5a4b-b420-08bb7d248ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267152} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbd4b54f-5a7e-5db0-b3a7-cdc2ebd91e2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c5084be9-ec71-5d1f-a612-cc093e9f2408" :question/math-academy-id "q-137131" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ {x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2} &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $3a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -8\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -4 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -4 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}6 & -24 \\\\ -24 & 96\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -32\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -24 & 8 \\\\ -24 & 96 & -32\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -24 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{4}{3}+4{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{3}+4{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n3a+\\frac{b}{c}=3\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{4}{1}=8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326895 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9642ac63-6925-5ea3-925d-e697a6222064" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form with Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10eb91a6-2d3a-53b3-b073-a10d66decb1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324127} {:answer/id #uuid "398c42e6-61f6-5edb-a6af-56df8a41a08f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324439} {:answer/id #uuid "70e7b5cd-ce67-5487-8c59-0136c6664fc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324901} {:answer/id #uuid "73b39860-f650-5a09-8af5-6cb9c76b62c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324931} {:answer/id #uuid "9fba6c6f-ee94-52be-9456-59da4a7eee4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "398c42e6-61f6-5edb-a6af-56df8a41a08f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324439} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b1a7497-9e7d-5e11-87dd-d6334e26484a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c3df77ba-fc93-5eb8-9827-0c8cef3d9027" :question/math-academy-id "q-40923" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $-10a+\\frac{6b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 6 & 3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}90 & -30 \\\\ -30 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-27 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}90 & -30 & -27 \\\\ -30 & 10 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}90 & -30 & -27 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have  \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{10}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{10}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{10} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n-10a+\\frac{6b}{c}=-10\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{10})+\\frac{6\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3})}{1}=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326896 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133801 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9642ac63-6925-5ea3-925d-e697a6222064" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form with Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03a2e5c9-1ec3-53d7-9d65-67e652469f37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324010} {:answer/id #uuid "48074d8b-44cf-5ab6-976d-e49c216938d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324554} {:answer/id #uuid "5dad58d2-2933-50ed-bf94-504e98ebf78f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324749} {:answer/id #uuid "c15de438-95e3-56a6-9a64-f99e256f9c2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325633} {:answer/id #uuid "e20db4f3-7b86-5a65-991a-7ed79cec5074" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325952}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5dad58d2-2933-50ed-bf94-504e98ebf78f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324749} :answer-field/id #uuid "74ca8095-e929-5df1-909d-a6fa762d4c42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b6fc54b-cb36-5749-9df6-d5883bd36fd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-40927" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 10\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $-2a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}30 & -60 \\\\ -60 & 120\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-15 \\\\ 30\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -2 & 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\n Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n-2a+\\frac{b}{c}=-2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{2}{1}=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134372 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133822 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fda08f5d-4513-5be0-b8c4-475e82f9bf5a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Normal Equation Corresponding to a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bcce621-ad6c-5e38-92ce-59257d82b2e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324207} {:answer/id #uuid "3ae637f1-21bd-58c0-8588-1f56b6ddf620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324445} {:answer/id #uuid "4f41b66f-c2b8-5e09-b24b-82987c5cd64f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324621} {:answer/id #uuid "7508b388-8184-5a28-8894-9eb4c9ab46c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324944} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ac2159-63e1-59da-a0e5-00c6faec0140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f41b66f-c2b8-5e09-b24b-82987c5cd64f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324621} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcdd0b61-b5b9-5f31-8491-d5721c2ec5db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "80e97aa1-dac8-53af-afe4-09e324e932eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-41909" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}7{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=5 \\\\ 14{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}245 & 70 & a \\\\ 70 & 20 & 10 \\\\ c & 10 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}35 \\\\ b \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations and its corresponding normal equation shown above. What is the value of $a+b+c?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 14 & 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe normal equation corresponding to $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}7 & 14 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 14 & 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 14 \\\\ 2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}245 & 70 & 35 \\\\ 70 & 20 & 10 \\\\ 35 & 10 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}35 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=35+10+35=80.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133802 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bc1e31e-5794-5ae3-8620-6bcaadde6a15" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System With Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "195f5438-dbcb-5fdf-b558-bce11648bd92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324187} {:answer/id #uuid "34da3d3b-8b0d-5731-9512-7880150858c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324401} {:answer/id #uuid "acc58718-2cff-5c4d-a0a5-bc1a9d33067c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325439} {:answer/id #uuid "cd585b41-d5de-5636-9761-c198aae3b03f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325751} {:answer/id #uuid "edef752f-66e7-582a-9d21-188b3b7ac00b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd585b41-d5de-5636-9761-c198aae3b03f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325751} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c9ed528-04bf-5ec5-8933-3ee5c01fd349" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00e4806c-b4ce-579d-8d0e-03b84320da38" :question/math-academy-id "q-41919" :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=1 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $30a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}30 & -30 \\\\ -30 & 30\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}30 & -30 & 1 \\\\ -30 & 30 & -1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}30 & -30 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{30}+{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{30}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{30} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n30a+\\frac{b}{c}=30\\cdot \\frac{1}{30}+\\frac{1}{1}=2.\n$$"}]}
